------------------------------ Protected routes with guards ----------------------------------------

- LET'S USE GUARDS TO PROTECT PARTS OF OUR APP.

- Instruction: Angular Prerequisite "Router" chapter (Advanced routing section)

1️⃣ 🔥All the pages related to races should only be accessible if the user is logged in. ⚠️There is
    no link to any of these pages if the user is not logged in, but that doesn’t prevent anyone to
    enter their URL in the location bar. 💡We would like to redirect to the home page (which has
    links to the login and register pages) if a user tries to access any of the routes under races.

    That’s what a canActivate guard is for. Generate a loggedInGuard functional guard using:

        ng generate guard logged-in --guardType=CanActivate


2️⃣ The guard must allow the navigation if the user is logged in. If the user is not logged in, it must
    prevent the navigation, and navigate to the home page. To redirect the user, you can inject the
    Router service in your guard (using inject), and return the new URL by building it using the
    parseUrl method. To check if the user is logged in, use the currentUser from the user service
    and check if it is not undefined.

        export const loggedInGuard: CanActivateFn = () => {
            const router = inject(Router);
            const userService = inject(UserService);

            return userService.currentUser() !== undefined || router.parseUrl('/');
        };


3️⃣ Now let's apply this loggedInGuard tho the routes related to the races.

        {
            path: 'races',
            canActivate: [loggedInGuard],
            children: [
                { path: '', component: Races },
                { path: ':raceId', component: Bet },
                { path: ':raceId/live', component: Live },
            ]
        }


4️⃣ Do not forget to copy all the test files and run ng test to be sure that everything is OK.


