Explanation: specifically let's understand the expression:

    return userService.currentUser() !== undefined || router.parseUrl('/');


👉 userService.currentUser() !== undefined

    First we should know that userService.currentUser() returns a signal value of type
    UserModel | undefined
    So, if we do:

        return userService.currentUser() || router.parseUrl('/');
    
    ⚠️ we will have an error because userService.currentUser() returns a signal value of type
    UserModel | undefined. It must return a boolean.
    Therefore 
        
        userService.currentUser() !== undefined

    is required because it produces a boolean.

    ✅If a user of UserModel exists then it produces true 

        userService.currentUser()
        // { id: 1, login: 'john' }

        userService.currentUser() !== undefined
        // true

    ✅ if undefined then false

        userService.currentUser()
        // undefined

        userService.currentUser() !== undefined
        // false


👉 return userService.currentUser() || router.parseUrl('/');

    ✅ The || operator means: "Return the first truthy value. If the first value is falsy, evaluate
        and return the second value."

    ✅ Case 1: User exists
        
            userService.currentUser() !== undefined
            // true
            return true || router.parseUrl('/');

        Since the left side is already true, JavaScript does not even evaluate the right side.
        Result:

            return true;
    
    ✅ Case 2: No user
            
            userService.currentUser() !== undefined
            // false
            return false || router.parseUrl('/');

        Now JavaScript must evaluate the right side:

            router.parseUrl('/');

        This creates a UrlTree representing the / route.
        Result:

            return UrlTree('/');

        🔥Angular interprets a returned UrlTree as: "Cancel the current navigation and redirect to this
        URL." So the user is redirected to /.